{{- /*
=============================================================================
configure-k9s-skin — idempotent ~/Library/Application Support/k9s/config.yaml patcher

Sets the `k9s.ui.skin` key in k9s's config.yaml to `tokyo-night-moon` (the
basename of the skin file deployed by
Library/Application Support/k9s/skins/tokyo-night-moon.yml.tmpl, which chezmoi
renders to ~/Library/Application Support/k9s/skins/tokyo-night-moon.yml —
k9s's native macOS default config location).

k9s 0.51+ schema note: skin selection lives at `k9s.ui.skin` (nested under the
required `k9s` object), NOT at a top-level `skin:` key. The older top-level
`skin:` form (from the axkirillov/k9s-tokyonight convention) is rejected by
k9s 0.51 with `Additional property skin is not allowed`, which makes k9s
fall back to the default skin. This script writes the schema-valid path and
removes any obsolete top-level `skin:` that an earlier (buggy) version of
this script may have written.

Why a script, not a pure `*.tmpl` file target: k9s writes runtime state
(current context, bookmarks, cluster-specific configs under clusters/) into
config.yaml and rewrites it during a session. A template target fully
overwrites the destination, which would clobber that runtime state — the same
hazard as ~/.docker/config.json. The yq merge preserves all existing fields
and only sets `k9s.ui.skin` (idempotent — fixed value, leaves every other key
intact) plus removes a stale top-level `skin` if present.

The skin file itself is never rewritten by k9s at runtime, so it is owned as a
pure template target (AGENTS.md Case A). Only config.yaml is patched here.

`run_onchange_before_` semantics:
  • `onchange_` runs only when THIS SCRIPT'S source content has changed
    (chezmoi state hash), so re-applies don't churn config.yaml's mtime once
    the patch is stable.
  • `before_` runs during step 4 of the application order, before the skin
    template target renders in step 5 — fine, because k9s only reads the skin
    file at launch time, by which point both the rendered skin and the merged
    config exist.
  • macOS-only (k9s's native macOS default location is used). The macOS arm of
    the install script installs k9s; no Linux arm exists in this build yet.

No K9S_CONFIG_DIR env var is used — targeting the native location means k9s
finds its skin regardless of how it is launched (GUI, existing shell, fresh
shell), so the theme is not shell-dependent.
=============================================================================
*/ -}}
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

SKIN_NAME="tokyo-night-moon"
K9S_DIR="$HOME/Library/Application Support/k9s"
CONFIG_FILE="$K9S_DIR/config.yaml"

echo "==> Configuring k9s skin ($SKIN_NAME) in $CONFIG_FILE..."
mkdir -p "$K9S_DIR"
if [ ! -f "$CONFIG_FILE" ]; then
  # k9s 0.51+ schema requires a top-level `k9s` object; seed with one so the
  # first yq merge produces a schema-valid file.
  echo "k9s: {}" > "$CONFIG_FILE"
fi

# Portable merge: yq (mikefarah/yq, the brew formula) writes to stdout, so
# render to a tmp file and atomically replace. Sets `k9s.ui.skin` to a fixed
# value and removes any obsolete top-level `skin` (written by older k9s or by
# a buggy prior version of this script), preserving every other key
# (currentContext, bookmarks, etc.).
tmp=$(mktemp)
yq 'del(.skin) | .k9s.ui.skin = "'"$SKIN_NAME"'"' "$CONFIG_FILE" > "$tmp" && mv "$tmp" "$CONFIG_FILE"

echo "    skin -> $SKIN_NAME"
{{- else -}}
{{- /* No-op on non-macOS. */ -}}
{{- end -}}